WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.520
- [Narrator] This is the
Hubble Space Telescope

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.200
that has revolutionized how
we look at the universe.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.880
And here is its successor,

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.600
the James Webb Space Telescope.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:10.540
It's the largest

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.120
and most powerful space
telescope ever built,

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.550
and it's about to launch later this month.

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:16.550
- You could almost think of it

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:20.150
as a telescope to complete
this grand question

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.270
of when did the first galaxies form?

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.600
- [Announcer] Liftoff of
the Space Shuttle Discovery

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.577
with the Hubble Space Telescope,

00:00:26.577 --> 00:00:28.230
our window on the universe.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.380
- [Narrator] The once
trailblazing Hubble Telescope,

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.000
launched in 1990,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.390
and has been orbiting Earth ever since,

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:36.110
capturing visuals like this,

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:39.100
a composite of filtered
and colorized images.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.410
Its ability to see light

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:41.970
from billions of years ago

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.140
has opened a window into
early parts of the universe

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.150
but it could never see
the very oldest galaxies.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.420
But now the James Webb Telescope

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.410
has the necessary technology

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.090
to capture the earliest
forming stars and planets,

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:58.843
and possibly a glimpse
into how life first began.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.350
The James Webb Telescope,

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.840
named after the former NASA administrator,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.650
is one of the largest
international scientific efforts

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:07.840
in US history.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.240
- Learning about the sky tells us more

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.900
about how our planet, how we came to be.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.350
- [Narrator] For over three decades,

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.880
Dr. Eric Smith has been
innovating telescopes for NASA.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:20.200
After working on Hubble,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.350
he then spent the last
25 years developing Webb.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:24.300
- You can make the case

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.960
that the history of 20th century astronomy

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.980
was building ever-larger telescopes

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.130
to see ever-farther galaxies.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:32.700
- [Narrator] The Hubble Space Telescope

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.960
takes images of planets,
stars and galaxies

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.930
some billions of light-years away

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.660
while staying in Earth's orbit

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.190
where it can be repaired.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.260
Hubble primarily scans the cosmos

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.900
in the same way our eyes
process visible light,

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.920
which enables it to view
hotter, younger stars.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.930
Colder stars, which can be billions

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.830
of years older are less visible to Hubble

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:57.410
and this is where the James
Webb Telescope will shine.

00:01:57.410 --> 00:01:58.770
Webb will observe the universe

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.290
in a different way than Hubble

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.090
using primary infrared light,

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.420
which isn't visible to the human eye.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:05.720
- When you look in the infrared,

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.300
you're studying things
that are a little cooler,

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:09.980
so you see cooler stars

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:13.070
of which there are many
more than the hotter stars.

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:16.020
It's also easier to detect faint things,

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.660
like planets and exoplanets.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.570
- [Narrator] Infrared
light allows the telescope

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:22.310
to unveil thick clouds of dust

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:24.380
that surround galaxies and nebula,

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.240
revealing more detail and information

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.040
about how stars and planets first formed.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.410
Here are two images of the same nebula

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:32.630
that Hubble took,

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:36.240
one using visible light and
the other using infrared.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.590
But James Webb will be able

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.310
to take infrared images
with even more clarity,

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:42.230
and sensitivity than Hubble,

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:43.760
and this will help it accomplish one

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.240
of its core missions,

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.070
looking back further in time
to the earliest galaxies

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:50.050
that formed after the big bang.

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:54.420
- The way Webb will be used
to see these first galaxies,

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.730
you take pictures of
different patches of the sky,

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:01.780
and you count the number
of faint galaxies you see

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:04.030
down to a certain brightness level.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.280
And at some point,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.430
you would expect to see a cliff,

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:10.430
saying there are just no
more galaxies below this.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:11.970
- [Narrator] The very faintest objects

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:12.803
that Webb will see

00:03:12.803 --> 00:03:15.360
are some of the oldest
lights in the universe,

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.350
revealing a window back in time.

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:19.130
And what powers this new telescope

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:21.800
to see galaxies that formed
billions of years ago

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.260
is cutting-edge technology.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.560
The $10 billion James Webb Telescope

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:28.990
is 100 times more powerful than Hubble

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:30.380
and much of that power comes

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.320
from its massive primary mirror,

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.320
which is over 21 feet wide,

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.560
nearly three times larger than Hubble's.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.210
It will be the largest ever put in space.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:41.240
And part of what makes it so powerful

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.720
is what the mirror
segments are composed of.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:45.216
- They're made of beryllium,

00:03:45.216 --> 00:03:47.580
which is a very light weight material

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.500
that's stable at the
very cold temperatures

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:52.750
we want the telescope to operate at.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:55.260
And then because we wanna
study the infrared part

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.850
of the spectrum, we wanna
have the smooth surface coated

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.980
with something that reflects
infrared light really well,

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.550
and that substance is gold.

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.420
It's actually a very thin layer.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:07.840
It's only about 1,000 atoms thick.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.650
If you scraped up all the gold

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:11.740
that was in Webb, it would
only be about the size

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:13.060
of a golf ball.

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:14.740
- [Narrator] Webb also
has advanced sensors

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.430
and scientific instruments onboard

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:19.010
to help scientists analyze the cosmos.

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.960
One instrument is the near
infrared spectrograph,

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.360
which is designed to determine

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.490
what kind of elements
or chemical compounds

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:27.360
are in distant parts of space.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.970
- In the big bang, we
had hydrogen and helium.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:31.320
Pretty much nothing else.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.590
Everything else was made in stars.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:35.640
So if you see a spectrum of an object

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.800
that has pretty much just
only hydrogen and helium,

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:41.620
then you know you're seeing
one of the first objects

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:42.720
to be formed.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.430
- [Narrator] And this is key.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:46.110
One goal of Webb is to give insight

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:47.780
into when it would have been possible

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.850
for habitable planets to first form,

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:50.970
which could hold clues

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:54.160
into how the universe
and life first began.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.840
For Webb sensors and
scientific instruments to work,

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.470
they need to be kept cool,

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.180
which is why a huge sun shield

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:03.510
with layers the thickness
of a plastic bag is used.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:06.540
And to see some of the
universe's oldest galaxies,

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.410
Webb will also need a crystal clear view.

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:10.780
This is why the telescope

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:12.920
will travel about a million miles away

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.800
from Earth towards deep space

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.240
where it cannot be
repaired like Hubble can.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:20.825
- Most of our space telescopes
orbit the Earth nearby.

00:05:20.825 --> 00:05:23.679
So why do we have to go
a million miles away?

00:05:23.679 --> 00:05:27.820
Primarily, it's to be far from the Earth,

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:31.550
which itself is a very bright
object in the infrared.

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:33.100
- [Narrator] As advanced as Webb is,

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:34.750
it won't be the last telescope

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:36.840
to search for life beyond Earth.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.870
A recent report from the National Academy

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:42.160
of Sciences recommended that
an even larger telescope

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:43.890
should be designed in the next decade

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:46.210
to look for life on exoplanets,

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.110
opening the window of possibility

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:51.116
for finding extraterrestrial life.

00:05:51.116 --> 00:05:53.783
(pensive music)

